#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:0
msgid ""
"Set up Ubuntu Desktop accessibility features for visual, hearing and "
"mobility impairments, including the Orca screen reader and keyboard options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:7
msgid "Topics: Accessibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:11
msgid "Accessibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:13
msgid ""
"Ubuntu aims at making the operating system, and its derivatives, usable by "
"as many people as possible across ages, languages and physical abilities. "
"This includes providing an accessible platform with high quality assistive "
"tools, and ensuring that other applications work well with these. The system "
"includes assistive technologies to support users with various impairments "
"and special needs, and to interact with common assistive devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:22
msgid "Visual impairments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:24
msgid "Blindness"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:26
msgid ""
"Orca is screen reading software suitable for vision impaired and blind users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/accessibility/index.md:28
msgid ""
"Orca is an assistive technology for people with visual impairments. Using "
"various combinations of speech synthesis and Braille, Orca helps provide "
"access to applications and toolkits that support the assistive technology "
"service provider interface (AT-SPI), e.g. the GNOME desktop."
msgstr ""

